Let \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
2x + 7 & \text{if } x < -2, \\
-x^2 - x + 1 & \text{if } x \ge -2.
\end{array}
\right.\]Find the sum of all values of $x$ such that $f(x) = -5.$

answer_2:
We are asked to find all values of $x$ such that $f(x) = -5$. \begin{align*}
f(x) = -5 \implies \text{either } 2x + 7 = -5 \text{ (if } x < -2\text{), or } -x^2 - x + 1 = -5 \text{ (if } x \ge -2\text{)}.
\end{align*} For $x < -2$: \begin{align*} 2x + 7 = -5 \implies 2x = -12 \implies x = -6. \end{align*} For $x \ge -2$: \begin{align*} -x^2 - x + 1 = -5 \implies -x^2 - x + 6 = 0 \implies x^2 + x - 6 = 0 \implies (x+3)(x-2) = 0. \end{align*} This gives $x = -3$ or $x = 2$. The values of $x$ that satisfy $f(x) = -5$ are $x = -6, -3,$ and $2$. The sum of these values is \begin{align*} -6 + (-3) + 2 = -7. \end{align*} \boxed{-7}

