Compute the ordered pair of positive integers $(x,y)$ such that

\begin{align*}
x^y+1&=y^x,\\
2x^y&=y^x+7.
\end{align*}

answer_1:
We are given the system of equations:
\begin{align*}
x^y+1&=y^x,\\
2x^y&=y^x+7.
\end{align*}
Subtracting the first equation from the second gives:
\begin{align*}
2x^y - (x^y+1) &= (y^x+7) - y^x \\
x^y - 1 &= 7 \\
x^y &= 8
\end{align*}
Since $x$ and $y$ are positive integers, $x^y = 8$ implies that $x=2$ and $y=3$, because $2^3=8$.
Substituting $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the first equation:
\begin{align*}
2^3+1&=3^2 \\
8+1&=9 \\
9&=9
\end{align*}
This is true, so the ordered pair is $\boxed{(2,3)}$.

