We are to count the number of distinct triangles formed by choosing any three distinct vertices of a cube. A cube has \(8\) vertices. The number of ways to choose \(3\) vertices from \(8\) is given by the binomial coefficient: \[
\binom{8}{3} = \frac{8!}{3!(8-3)!} = \frac{8 \cdot 7 \cdot 6}{3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} = 56.
\] Thus, there are \(56\) distinct triangles. \boxed{56}

