We are given that $f(x) + g(x) = -2 + x$ and $f(x) = x^3 - 2x - 2$.  To find $g(x)$, we need to isolate $g(x)$.  We can do this by subtracting $f(x)$ from both sides of the equation $f(x) + g(x) = -2 + x$: $$\begin{align*} g(x) &= -2 + x - f(x) \\ &= -2 + x - (x^3 - 2x - 2). \end{align*}$$ Now, distribute the negative sign: $$\begin{align*} g(x) &= -2 + x - x^3 + 2x + 2 \\ &= -x^3 + 3x + 0. \end{align*}$$ Simplifying, we get: $$g(x) = -x^3 + 3x$$To find \( g(x) \), subtract \( f(x) \) from both sides of the equation \( f(x) + g(x) = -2 + x \):
\begin{align*}
g(x) &= -2 + x - f(x) \\
&= -2 + x - (x^3 - 2x - 2) \\
&= -2 + x - x^3 + 2x + 2 \\
&= -x^3 + 3x
\end{align*}
Therefore, \( g(x) = -x^3 + 3x \). \boxed{-x^3 + 3x}

