To find the number of distinct solutions to the equation $|x-7| = |x+1|$, we need to consider the cases where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign.1.  Identify the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign.  These are \(x=7\) and \(x=-1\).2.  Consider the four cases based on the sign of these expressions:
Case 1: \(x > 7\)
\begin{align*}
|x-7| &= x-7 \\
|x+1| &= x+1
\end{align*}
Equating the absolute values:
\begin{align*}
x-7 &= x+1 \\
-7 &= 1 \quad \text{(False)}
\end{align*}
No solutions in this case.
Case 2: \(7 \ge x > -1\)
\begin{align*}
|x-7| &= 7-x \\
|x+1| &= x+1
\end{align*}
Equating the absolute values:
\begin{align*}
7-x &= x+1 \\
7-1 &= 2x \\
6 &= 2x \\
x &= 3
\end{align*}
This is a valid solution.
Case 3: \(-1 \ge x > -\infty\)
\begin{align*}
|x-7| &= 7-x \\
|x+1| &= -(x+1) = -x-1
\end{align*}
Equating the absolute values:
\begin{align*}
7-x &= -x-1 \\
7 &= -1 \quad \text{(False)}
\end{align*}
No solutions in this case.
Case 4: \(x < -1\)
\begin{align*}
|x-7| &= 7-x \\
|x+1| &= -(x+1) = -x-1
\end{align*}
Equating the absolute values:
\begin{align*}
7-x &= -x-1 \\
7 &= -1 \quad \text{(False)}
\end{align*}
No solutions in this case.3.  Summarizing the solutions found:
- 1 solution in Case 2: \(x = 3\)
- 0 solutions in Cases 1, 3, and 4.4.  Therefore, the number of distinct solutions is \boxed{1}.

