To find $\cos 240^\circ$, we can use the unit circle and trigonometric identities.
First, we note that $240^\circ$ is in the third quadrant, where cosine values are negative.
We can also write $240^\circ$ as $180^\circ + 60^\circ$.
Using the identity $\cos (180^\circ + \theta) = -\cos \theta$, we get:
\begin{align*}
\cos 240^\circ &= \cos (180^\circ + 60^\circ) \\
&= -\cos 60^\circ \\
&= -\frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}
Therefore, the answer is \boxed{-\dfrac{1}{2}}.

