First, complete the square for the quadratic expression. We have:
\begin{align*}
6j^2 - 4j + 12 &= 6\left(j^2 - \frac{4}{6}j + 2\right) \\
&= 6\left(j^2 - \frac{2}{3}j + \left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^2 - \left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^2 + 2\right) \\
&= 6\left(\left(j - \frac{1}{3}\right)^2 - \frac{1}{9} + 2\right) \\
&= 6\left(\left(j - \frac{1}{3}\right)^2 + \frac{17}{9}\right) \\
&= 6\left(j - \frac{1}{3}\right)^2 + 6\left(\frac{17}{9}\right) \\
&= 6\left(j - \frac{1}{3}\right)^2 + \frac{17}{3}.
\end{align*}
Comparing this to the form $c(j + p)^2 + q$, we see that $c = 6$, $p = -\frac{1}{3}$, and $q = \frac{17}{3}$. Therefore, $\frac{q}{p} = \frac{\frac{17}{3}}{-\frac{1}{3}} = \frac{17}{3} \times \left(-\frac{3}{1}\right) = -17$. \boxed{-17}

