answer_1:
We are given a bowl with 10 jellybeans: 4 red, 1 blue, and 5 white. We need to find the probability of picking exactly 2 red jellybeans when we pick 3 jellybeans at random and without replacement.

First, calculate the total number of ways to pick 3 jellybeans from the 10 available:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Total ways} &= \binom{10}{3} = \frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \frac{10 \times 9 \times 8}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 120
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Next, calculate the number of favorable outcomes (ways to pick exactly 2 red and 1 non-red jellybean):
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Favorable ways} &= \binom{4}{2} \times \binom{6}{1} \\
&= \frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} \times \frac{6!}{1!(6-1)!} \\
&= \frac{4 \times 3}{2 \times 1} \times 6 \\
&= 6 \times 6 \\
&= 36
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Finally, calculate the probability of exactly 2 red jellybeans:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Probability} &= \frac{\text{Favorable ways}}{\text{Total ways}} \\
&= \frac{36}{120} \\
&= \frac{3}{10}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\boxed{\frac{3}{10}}

