First, we have two equations:
\[
\begin{aligned}
2x - y &= 5 \quad \text{(Equation 1)} \\
x + 2y &= 5 \quad \text{(Equation 2)}
\end{aligned}
\]
To solve this system of equations, we can use the method of elimination. To eliminate $y$, we can multiply Equation 1 by 2:
\[
\begin{aligned}
2(2x - y) &= 2(5) \\
4x - 2y &= 10 \quad \text{(Equation 3)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Now, we add Equation 3 to Equation 2:
\[
\begin{aligned}
4x - 2y + x + 2y &= 10 + 5 \\
5x &= 15 \\
x &= \frac{15}{5} \\
x &= 3
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, the value of $x$ is $\boxed{3}$.
The answer is: \boxed{3}

