Let \[f(n) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
n^2-2 & \text{ if }n<0, \\
2n-20 & \text{ if }n \geq 0.
\end{array}
\right.\]What is the positive difference between the two values of $a$ that satisfy the equation $f(-2)+f(2)+f(a)=0$?

answer_2:
We want to find the number of functions $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying
\begin{align*}
f(x + f(y)) &= x + y
\end{align*}for all real $x$ and $y.$ To start, set $y = 0$ in the original equation. This gives
\begin{align*}
f(x + f(0)) &= x.
\end{align*}If we let $a = f(0),$ then the equation becomes
\begin{align*}
f(x + a) &= x.
\end{align*}Now, set $x = 0$ in this equation to get
\begin{align*}
f(a) &= 0.
\end{align*}Combining this with the first equation, we have
\begin{align*}
f(x + 0) &= x,
\end{align*}so $f(x) = x$ for all $x$ in the domain. Let's verify this function satisfies the original equation. Substituting $f(x) = x$ into the original equation, we get
\begin{align*}
f(x + f(y)) &= x + y.
\end{align*}Since $f(y) = y,$ this becomes
\begin{align*}
f(x + y) &= x + y,
\end{align*}which is true. Thus, the only function satisfying the given equation is $f(x) = x,$ and there is exactly one such function. Therefore, the answer is \boxed{1}.

