other_correct:
We will evaluate \(f\) repeatedly starting with \(f(1)\).  First, evaluate \(f\) at \(1\):  \[f(1)=3(1)+1=4.\] Next, evaluate \(f\) at \(4\):  \[f(4)=\frac{4}{2}=2.\] Next, evaluate \(f\) at \(2\):  \[f(2)=\frac{2}{2}=1.\] Finally, evaluate \(f\) at \(1\) again to get \(f(f(f(f(1))))\):  \[f(f(f(f(1))))=f(f(f(1)))=f(f(2))=f(1)=4.\] Thus, \(f(f(f(f(1))))=4\). \[
\boxed{4}
\]

