answer_2:
We have the equation:
\begin{align*}
P(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1) &= 0 \\
(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)^3 - P(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1) &= 0 \\
(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)^3 - P(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1) &= 0
\end{align*}
Using the binomial theorem, we expand $(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)^3$:
\begin{align*}
(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1)^3 &= (\sqrt[3]{2})^3 + 3(\sqrt[3]{2})^2(1) + 3(\sqrt[3]{2})(1)^2 + (1)^3 \\
&= 2 + 3(\sqrt[3]{4}) + 3\sqrt[3]{2} + 1 \\
&= 3 + 3\sqrt[3]{2} + 3(\sqrt[3]{2})^2
\end{align*}
Substituting this back into our equation:
\begin{align*}
3 + 3\sqrt[3]{2} + 3(\sqrt[3]{2})^2 - P(\sqrt[3]{2} + 1) &= 0
\end{align*}
To express this in the form $P(x) = 0$, we need to write $\sqrt[3]{2}$ in terms of $x$:
\begin{align*}
\sqrt[3]{2} &= x - 1 \\
(\sqrt[3]{2})^2 &= (x - 1)^2 = x^2 - 2x + 1
\end{align*}
Substituting these into the previous equation:
\begin{align*}
3 + 3(x - 1) + 3(x^2 - 2x + 1) - P(x) &= 0 \\
3 + 3x - 3 + 3x^2 - 6x + 3 - P(x) &= 0 \\
3x^2 - 3x + 3 - P(x) &= 0
\end{align*}
Rearranging to solve for $P(x)$:
\begin{align*}
P(x) &= 3x^2 - 3x + 3 \\
P(x) &= x^2 - x + 1
\end{align*}
Therefore, the monic cubic polynomial is $x^2 - x + 1$.
\boxed{x^2 - x + 1}

